have no courage to deny the }{\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 Vedas, }{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 although indirectly they disobey all the injunctions of the }{\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 Vedas. }{
\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 Lord Caitanya condemned this.
\par The example of the conchshell and that of the cowdung given by the Lord are very much appropriate in this connection. If one argues that since cowdung is pure, the stool of a learned }{\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 br\'e4hma\'eba }{
\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 is still more pure, his argument will not be accepted. Cowdung is accepted, and the stool of a highly posted }{\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 br\'e4hma\'eba }{
