sadeva's compilation of different Vedic literatures on the basis of regulated performances of fruitive activities as depicted in the }{\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 Mah\'e4bh\'e4rata }{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 
and other literature is condemned herewith by \'c7r\'e9la N\'e4rada. The hu
man beings, by long material association, life after life, have a natural inclination, by practice, to endeavor to lord it over material energy. They have no sense of the responsibility of human life. This human form of life is a chance to get out of the 
clutches of illusory matter. The }{\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 Vedas }{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 
are meant for going back to Godhead, going back home. To revolve in the cycle of transmigration in a series of lives numbering 8,400,000 is an imprisoned life for the condemned conditioned souls. The human form of life
 is a chance to get out of this imprisoned life, and as such the only occupation of the human being is to reestablish his lost relationship with God. Under the circumstances, one should never be encouraged in making a plan for sense enjoyment in the name 
of religious functions. Such diversion of the human energy results in a misguided civilization. \'c7r\'e9la Vy\'e4sadeva is the authority in Vedic explanations in the }{\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 Mah\'e4bh\'e4rata, }{
\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 etc., and his encouragement in sense enjoyment in some form or other is a great barrie
r for spiritual advancement because the people in general will not agree to renounce material activities which hold them in material bondage. At a certain stage of human civilization, such material activities in the name of religion (as sacrificing animal
s in the name of }{\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 yaj\'efa) }{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 were too much rampant. The Lord incarnated Himself as Buddha and decried the authority of the }{
\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 Vedas }{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 to stop animal sacrifice in the name of religion. This was foreseen by N\'e4rada, and therefore he condemned such literatures. The flesh
-eaters still continue to perform animal sacrifice before some demigod or goddess in the name of religion because in some of the Vedic literatures such regulated sacrifice is recommended. They are so recommended to discourage flesh-eating, but gradually t
he purpose of such religious activities is forgotten, and the slaughterhouse becomes prominent. This is because foolish materialistic men do not care to listen to others who are actually in a position to explain the Vedic rites.
\par In the }{\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 Vedas }{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 it is distinctl
y said that the perfection of life is never to be attained either by voluminous work, or by accumulation of wealth or even by increasing the number of population. But it is so attained only by renunciation. The materialistic men do not care to listen to s
uch injunctions. According to them, the so-called renounced order of life is meant for those who are unable to earn their livelihood because of some corporeal defects, or for persons who have failed to achieve prosperity in family life.
\par In histories like the }{\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 Mah\'e4bh\'e4rata, }{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 of course, there are topics on transcendental subjects along with material topics. The }{
\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4 }{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 is there in the }{\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 Mah\'e4bh\'e4rata. }{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 
The whole idea of the }{\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 Mah\'e4bh\'e4rata }{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 is culminated in the ultimate instructions of the }{\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 Bhagavad-g\'e9t
\'e4, }{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 that one should relinquish all other engagements and should engage oneself solely and fully in surrendering unto the lotus feet of Lord \'c7r\'e9 K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a. But men with materialistic tendencies are more attracted to politics, economics and philanthropic activities mentioned in the }{\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 Mah\'e4bh\'e4rata }{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 
than to the principal topic, namely the }{\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4. }{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 This compromising spirit of Vy\'e4sadeva is directly condemned by N\'e4
rada, who advises him to directly proclaim that the prime necessity of human life is to realize one's eternal relation with the Lord and thus surrender unto Him without delay.
\par A patient suffering from a particular type of malady is almost always inclined to accept eatables which are forbidden for him. The expert physician does not make any compromise with the p
atient by allowing him to take partially what he should not at all take. In the }{\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4 }{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 
it is also said that a man attached to fruitive work should not be discouraged from his occupation, for gradually he may be elevated to the position of self-reali
zation. This is sometimes applicable for those who are only dry empiric philosophers without spiritual realization. But those who are in the devotional line need not be always so advised.
\par 
