 was to be killed as well as spared according to different scriptures cited by different persons. As a }{\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 brahma-bandhu, }{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 or a worthless son of a }{
\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 br\'e4hma\'eba. }{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 A\'e7vatth\'e4m\'e4 was not to be killed,
 but he was at the same time an aggressor also. And according to the rulings of Manu, an aggressor, even though he be a }{\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 br\'e4hma\'eba }{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 
(and what to speak of an unworthy son of a }{\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 br\'e4hma\'eba), }{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 is to be killed. Dro\'eb\'e4c\'e4rya was certainly a }{
\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 br\'e4hma\'eba }{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 in the true sense of the term, but because he stood in the battlefield he was killed. But although A\'e7vatth\'e4m\'e4
 was an aggressor, he stood without any fighting weapons. The ruling is that an aggressor, when he is without weapon or chariot, cannot be killed. All these were certa
inly perplexities. Besides that, Arjuna had to keep the promise he had made before Draupad\'e9 just to pacify her. But he also had to satisfy both Bh\'e9ma and K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a, who advised killing him. This dilemma was present before Arjuna, and the solution was awarded by K\'e5\'f1\'eba.
