of his time. But they left everything to gain something superior to what they previously possessed. The devotees are generally without material prosperity, but they have a very secret treasure house in the lotus feet of the Lord. There is}{
\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592  }{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid4212850 a nice story}{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592  about \'c7r\'e9la}{\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592  }{
\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 San\'e4tana Gosv\'e4m\'e9
. He had a touchstone with him, and this stone was left in a pile of refuse. A needy man took it, but later on wondered why the valuable stone was kept in such a neglected place. He therefore asked for the most valua
ble thing from him, and then he was given the holy name of the Lord. }{\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 Aki\'efcana }{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 means one who has nothing to give materially. A factual devotee or the }{
\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 mah\'e4tm\'e4 }{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 does not give anything material to anyone because he has already left all material assets. He can, 
however, deliver the supreme asset, namely the Personality of Godhead, because He is}{\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592  }{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 the only property of a factual devotee. The touchstone of San\'e4
tana Gosv\'e4m\'e9 which was thrown in the rubbish was not the property of the Gosv\'e4m\'e9, otherwise it would not have
 been kept in such a place. This specific example is given for the neophyte devotees just to convince them that material hankerings and spiritual advancement go ill together. Unless one is able to see everything as spiritual in relation with the Supreme L
ord, one must always distinguish between spirit and matter. A spiritual master like \'c7r\'e9la San\'e4tana Gosv\'e4m\'e9, although personally able to see everything as spiritual, set this example for us only because we have no such spiritual vision.

\par Advancement of materi
al vision or material civilization is a great stumbling block for spiritual advancement. Such material advancement entangles the living being in the bondage of a material body followed by all sorts of material miseries. Such material advancement is called
 }{\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 anartha }{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 
or things not wanted. Actually this is so. In the present context of material advancement one uses lipstick at a cost of fifty cents and there are so many unwanted things which are all products of the material conception of life. By diverting atten
tion to so many unwanted things, human energy is spoiled without achievement of spiritual realization, the prime necessity of human life. The attempt to reach the moon is another example of spoiling energy because even if the moon is reached, the problems
 of life will not be solved. The devotees of the Lord are called }{\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid1605015 aki}{\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid1605015\charrsid12326592 \'ef}{\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid1605015 canas}{
\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592  }{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 
because they have practically no material assets. Such material assets are all products of the three modes of material nature. They foil spiritual energy, and thus the less we possess such products of material nature, the more we have a good chance for sp
iritual progress.
\par The Supreme Personalil}{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid1605015 y}{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 
 of Godhead has no direct connection with material activities. All His acts and deeds, which are exhibited even in this material world, are spiritual and without affection of the modes of material nature. In the }{
\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4 }{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 
the Lord says that all His acts, even His appearence and disappearence in and out of the material world, are all transcendental, and one who knows this perfectly shall not take his birth again in this material world, but he will go back to Godhead.
\par The material disease is due to hankering after lording it over material nature. This hankering is due to an interaction of the three modes of nature, and both the Lord and the devotees have no attachment for such false enjoyment. Therefore, the Lord
 as well as the devotees are called }{\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 niv\'e5tta-gu\'eba-v\'e5tti. }{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 The perfect }{\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 niv\'e5tta-gu\'eba-v\'e5tti }{
\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 is the Supreme Lord because He never becomes attracted by the modes of material nature, whereas the living beings have such a tendency. Some of them are entrapped by the illusory attraction of material nature.

\par Because the Lord is the property of the devotees and the devotees are the property of the Lord, reciprocally the devotees are certainly transcendental to the modes of material nature. That is a natural conclusion. S
uch unalloyed devotees are distinct from the mixed devotees who approach the Lord for mitigation of miseries and poverty, inquisitiveness and speculation. The unalloyed devotees and the Lord are transcendentally attached to one another. For others, the Lo
rd has nothing to reciprocate, and therefore He is called }{\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 \'e4tm\'e4r\'e4ma, }{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 
self-satisfied. Self-satisfied as He is, He is the master of all monists who seek to merge into the existence of the Lord. Such monists merge within the personal effulgence of the Lord called the }{\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 
brahmajyoti, }{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 but the devotees enter into the transcendental pastimes of the Lord, which are never to he misunderstood as material.
