etra, especially on his account. Duryodhana was there on the throne, and he was doing well in his administration, and in one sense there was no need of fighting. But on the principle of justice Yudhi\'f1\'f6
hira was to be replaced. The whole clique of politics centered round this point, and all the kings and residents of the whole world became involved in this fight between the rival brothers. Lord K\'e5\'f1\'eba was also there in the side of King Yudhi\'f1
\'f6hira. It is said in the }{\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 Mah\'e4bh\'e4rata (\'c4di, }{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 Chap. 20) that 6,400,000 men were killed in eighteen days in the Battle of Kuruk\'f1
etra, and some hundreds of thousands were missing. Practically this was the greatest battle in the world within five thousand years.
\par This mass killing simply to enthrone Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Yudhi\'f1\'f6hira was too mortifying, so he tried to be convinced with evidences from histories by great sages like Vy\'e4
sa and the Lord Himself that the fight was just because the cause was just. But Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Yudhi\'f1\'f6hira would not be satisfied, even though he was instructed by the greatest personalities of the time. K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a is designated herein as the performer of superhuman actions, but in this particular instance neither He nor Vy\'e4sa could convince King Yudhi\'f1\'f6
hira. Does it mean that He failed to be a superhuman actor? No, certainly not. The interpretation is that the Lord as }{\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 \'e9\'e7vara }{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 
or the Supersoul both in the heart of King Yudhi\'f1\'f6hira and Vy\'e4sa performed still more superhuman action because the Lord desired it. As Supersoul of King Yudhi\'f1\'f6hira, He did not allow the King to be convinced by the words of Vy\'e4
sa and others, including Himself, because He desired that the King hear instructions from the dying Bh\'e9\'f1madeva, who was another great devotee of the Lord. The Lord wanted that at the last stage of his material existence the great warrior Bh\'e9\'f1
madeva see the Lord personally as well as his beloved grandchildren, King Yudhi\'f1\'f6hira, etc., now situated on the throne, and thus pass away very peacefully. Bh\'e9\'f1madeva was not at all satisfied to fight against the P\'e4\'eb\'f2
avas, who were his beloved fatherless grandchildren. But the }{\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 k\'f1atriyas }{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 are also very stern people, and therefo
re he was obliged to take the side of Duryodhana because he was maintained at the expense of Duryodhana. Besides this, the Lord also desired that King Yudhi\'f1\'f6hira be pacified by the words of Bh\'e9\'f1madeva so that the world could see that Bh\'e9
\'f1madeva excelled all in knowledge, including the Lord Himself.
