a by His own internal pleasure appears on earth, He does so by the agency of His internal potency. The attractive features of His transcendental body are desired in all the t
hree worlds, namely the upper, middle and lower planetary systems. Nowhere in the universe are there such beautiful bodily features as those of Lord K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a. Therefore His transcendental body has nothing to do with anything materially created. Arjuna is described here as the conquerer, and K\'e5\'f1\'eba is described as his intimate friend. Bh\'e9\'f1madeva on his bed of arrows after the Battle of Kuruk\'f1
etra is remembering the particular dress of Lord K\'e5\'f1\'eba which He put on as the driver of Arjuna's chariot. While fighting was going on between Arjuna and Bh\'e9\'f1ma, Bh\'e9\'f1ma's attraction was drawn by the glittering dress of K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a, and indirectly he admired his so-called enemy Arjuna for possessing the Lord as his friend. Arjuna was always a conquerer because the Lord was his friend. Bh\'e9\'f1madeva takes this opportunity to address the Lord as }{
\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 vijayasakhe }{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 
