me in a sporting spirit, like a friend fights with another friend. Duryodhana criticized Bh\'e9\'f1madeva, alleging that he was reluctant to kill Arjuna because of paternal affection. A }{\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 k\'f1atriya }{
\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 cannot tolerate insults on the principle of fighting. Bh\'e9\'f1madeva therefore promised that the next day he would kill all five P\'e4\'eb\'f2
avas with special weapons made for the purpose. Duryodhana was satisfied, and he kept the arrows with him to be delivered the next day during the fight. By tricks Arjuna took the arrows from Duryodhana, and Bh\'e9\'f1
madeva could understand that this was the trick of Lord K\'e5\'f1\'eba. So he took a vow that the next day K\'e5\'f1\'eba would have to take up weapons Himself, otherwise His friend Arjuna would die. So in the next day's fighting Bh\'e9\'f1
madeva fought so violently that both Arjuna and K\'e5\'f1\'eba were in trouble. Arjuna was almost defeated; the situation was so tense that he was about to be killed by Bh\'e9\'f1madeva the very next moment. At that time Lord K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a wanted to please His devotee, Bh\'e9\'f1ma, by keeping his promise, which was more important than His own. Seemingly He broke His own promise. He promised before the beginning of the Battle of Kuruk\'f1
etra that He would remain without weapons and would not use His strength for either of the parties. But to protect Arjuna He got down from the chariot, took up the wheel of the chariot and hurriedly rushed at Bh\'e9\'f1
madeva in an angry mood, like a lion goes to kill an elephant. He dropped His covering cloth on the way, and out of great anger He did not know that He dropped it. Bh\'e9\'f1madeva at once gave up his weapons and stood to }{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid15479592 b
}{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 e killed by K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a, his beloved Lord. The fighting of the day was thus ended at that very moment, and Arjuna was saved. Of course there was no possibility of Arjuna's death because the Lord Himself was on the chariot, but because Bh\'e9\'f1madeva wanted to see Lord K\'e5
\'f1\'eba take up some weapon to save His friend, the Lord created this situation, making Arjuna's death imminent. He stood before Bh\'e9\'f1madeva to show him that his promise was fulfilled and that He had taken up the wheel.
