hira, even up to the last moment of his life. He died very happily by seeing Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Yudhi\'f1\'f6hira to the throne, otherwise he would have long ago quitted his material body, instead of suffering agony over the undue sufferings of the P\'e4\'eb
\'f2avas. He was simply waiting for the opportune moment because he was sure and certain that the sons of P\'e4\'eb\'f2u would come out victorious in the Battlefield of Kuruk\'f1etra, as His Lordship \'c7r\'e9}{
\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592  }{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 K\'e5\'f1\'eba was their protector. As a devotee of the Lord, he knew that the Lord's devotee cannot be vanquished at any rate. Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Yudhi\'f1\'f6
hira was quite aware of all these good wishes of Bh\'e9\'f1madeva, and therefore he must have been feeling the great separation. He was sorry for the separation of a great soul, and not for the material body which Bh\'e9\'f1
madeva relinquished. The funeral ceremony was a necessary duty, although Bh\'e9\'f1madeva was a liberated soul. Since Bh\'e9\'f1madeva was without issue, the eldest grandson, namely Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Yudhi\'f1\'f6
hira, was the rightful person to perform this ceremony. It was a great boon to Bh\'e9\'f1madeva that an equally great son of the family undertook the last rites of a great man.
