, the father and the mother of Duryodhana and his brothers, were the elder uncle and aunt of Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Yudhi\'f1\'f6hira. After the Battle of Kuruk\'f1etra, the celebrated couple, having lost all their sons and grandsons, were under the care of Mah
\'e4r\'e4ja Yudhi\'f1\'f6hira. They were passing their days in great agony over such heavy loss of life and were practically living the life of ascetics. The death news of Bh\'e9\'f1madeva. uncle of Dh\'e5tar\'e4\'f1\'f6ra, was another great sh
ock for the King and the Queen, and therefore they required solace from Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Yudhi\'f1\'f6hira. Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Yudhi\'f1\'f6hira was conscious of his duty, and he at once hurried to the spot with Lord K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a and satisfied the begrieved Dh\'e5tar\'e4\'f1\'f6ra with kind words, both from himself and the Lord also.
\par G\'e4ndh\'e4r\'e9 was a powerful ascetic, although she was living the life of a faithful wife and a kind mother. It is said that G\'e4ndh\'e4r\'e9 also voluntarily closed her eyes because of the blindness of her husband. A wife's duty is to fo
llow the husband cent percent. And G\'e4ndh\'e4r\'e9
 was so true to her husband that she followed him even in his perpetual blindness. Therefore in her actions she was a great ascetic. Besides that, the shock which she suffered because of the wholesale killing of h
er one hundred sons and her grandsons also was certainly too much for a woman. But she suffered all this just like an ascetic. G\'e4ndh\'e4r\'e9, although a woman, is no less than Bh\'e9\'f1
madeva in character. They are both remarkable personalities in the }{\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 Mah\'e4bh\'e4rata.
