a in human society for all the suffering humanity of the world, and we simply request the leading personalities of all nations to take to the science of K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a for their own good, for the good of society, and for the good of all the people of the world." So it is confirmed herein by the example of Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Yudhi\'f1\'f6hira, the personality of goodness. In India the people hanker after }{
\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 R\'e4ma-r\'e4jya }{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 
because the Personality of Godhead was the ideal king and all other kings or emperors in India controlled the destiny of the world for the prosperity of every living being who took birth on the earth. Herein the word }{
\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 praj\'e4\'f9 }{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 
is significant. The etymological import of the word is "that which is born." On the earth there are many species of life, from the aquatics up to the perfect human beings, and all are known as }{\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 praj\'e4s. }
{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 Lord Brahm\'e4, the creator of this particular universe, is known as the }{\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 praj\'e4pati }{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 
because he is the grandfather of all who have taken birth. Thus }{\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 praj\'e4 }{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 
is used in a broader sense than it is now used. The king represents all living beings, the aquatics, plants, trees, the reptiles, the birds, the ani
mals and man. Every one of them is a part and parcel of the Supreme Lord (Bg. 14.4), and the king, being the representative of the Supreme Lord, is duty-bound to give proper protection to every one of them. This is not the case with the presidents and dic
tators of this demoralized system of administration where the lower animals are given no protection while the higher animals are given so-called protection. But this is a great science which can be learned only by one who knows the }{
\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 science of K\'e5\'f1\'eba. }{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 By knowing the science of K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a, one can become the most perfect man in the world, and unless one has knowledge in this science, all qualifications and doctorate diplomas acquired by academic education are spoiled and useless. Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Yudhi\'f1\'f6hira knew this science of K
\'e5\'f1\'eba very well, for it is stated here that by continuous cultivation of this science, or by continuous devotional service to Lord K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a, he acquired the qualification of administering the state. The father is sometimes seemingly cruel to the son, bu
t that does not mean that the father has lost the qualification to be a father. A father is always a father because he has always the good of the son at heart. The father wants every one of his sons to become a better man than himself. Therefore, a king l
ike Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Yudhi\'f1\'f6hira, who is the personality of goodness, wanted everyone under his administration, especially human beings who have better developed consciousness, to become devotees of Lord K\'e5\'f1\'eb
a so that everyone can become free from the trifles of ma
terial existence. His motto of administration was all good for the citizens, for as personified goodness, he knew perfectly well what is actually good for them. He conducted the administration on that principle, and not on the }{
\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 r\'e4k\'f1asi }{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 or demonic principle 
of sense gratification. As an ideal king, he had no personal ambition, and there was no place for sense gratification because all his senses at all times were engaged in the loving service of the Supreme Lord, which includes the partial service to the liv
i
ng beings who form the parts and parcels of the complete whole. Those who are busy rendering service to the parts and parcels, leaving aside the whole, only spoil time and energy, as one does when watering the leaves of a tree without watering the root. I
f water is poured on the root, the leaves are enlivened perfectly and automatically, but if water is poured on the leaves only, the whole energy is spoiled. Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Yudhi\'f1\'f6
hira, therefore, was constantly engaged in the service of the Lord, and thus the par
ts and parcels of the Lord, the living beings under his careful administration, were perfectly attended with all comforts in this life and all progress in the next. That is the way of perfect management of state administration.
\par 
