s death at an early age, his minor children and widow were the object of special care by all the elderly members of the family, especially Bh\'e9\'f1madeva and Mah\'e4tm\'e4 Vidura. Vidura was more or less partial to the P\'e4\'eb\'f2
avas due to their political position. Although Dh\'e5tar\'e4\'f1\'f6ra was equally careful for the minor children of Mah\'e4r\'e4ja P\'e4\'eb\'f2u, he was one of the intriguing parties who wanted to wash away the descendants of P\'e4\'eb\'f2
u and replace them by raising his own sons to become the rulers of the kingdom. Mah\'e4tm\'e4 Vidura could follow this intrigue of Dh\'e5tar\'e4\'f1\'f6ra and company, and therefore, even though he was a faithful servitor of his eldest brother Dh\'e5tar
\'e4\'f1\'f6ra, he did not like his political ambition for the sake of his own sons. He was therefore very careful about the protection of the P\'e4\'eb\'f2avas and their widow mother. Thus he was, so to speak, partial to the P\'e4\'eb\'f2
avas, preferring them to the sons of Dh\'e5tar\'e4\'f1\'f6ra, although both of them were equally affectionate in his ordinary eyes.
 He was equally affectionate to both the camps of nephews in the sense that he always chastised Duryodhana for his intriguing policy against his cousins. He always criticized his elder brother for his policy of encouragement to his sons, and at the same t
ime he was always alert in giving special protection to the P\'e4\'eb\'f2avas. All these different activities of Vidura within the palace politics made him well-known as partial to the P\'e4\'eb\'f2avas. Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Yudhi\'f1\'f6
hira has referred to the past history of Vidura before his going away from home for a prolonged pilgrim\rquote s journey. Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Yudhi\'f1\'f6
hira reminded him that he was equally kind and partial to his grown-up nephews, even after the Battle of Kuruk\'f1etra, a great family disaster.
\par Before the Battle of Kuruk\'f1etra, Dh\'e5tar\'e4\'f1\'f6ra\rquote s policy was peaceful annihilation of his nephews, and therefore he ordered Purocana to build a house at V\'e4ra\'eb}{\f35\fs28\insrsid481184 a}{\f35\fs28\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 
vat, and when the building was finished Dh\'e5tar\'e4\'f1\'f6ra desired that his brother\rquote s family live there for some time. When the P\'e4\'eb\'f2avas were goi
ng there in the presence of all the members of the royal family, Vidura tactfully gave instructions to the P\'e4\'eb\'f2avas about the future plan of Dh\'e5tar\'e4\'f1\'f6ra. This is specifically described in the }{
\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 Mah\'e4bh\'e4rata }{\f35\fs28\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 (Ch. 144 }{\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 \'c4di-parva). }{\f35\fs28\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 He indirectly hinted, }{
\i\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 \'93A weapon not made of steel or any other material element can be more than sharp to kill an enemy, and he who knows this is never killed.\'94 }{\f35\fs28\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 
That is to say, he hinted that the party of the P\'e4\'eb\'f2avas was being sent to }{\f35\fs28\insrsid481184 V\'e4ra\'ebavat}{\f35\fs28\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592  to be killed, and thus he warned Yudhi\'f1\'f6h
ira to be very careful in their new residential palace. He also gave indications of fire and said that fire cannot extinguish the soul, but it can annihilate the material body. But one who protects the soul can live. Kunt\'e9
 could not follow such indirect conversations between Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Yudhi\'f1\'f6hira and Vidura, and thus when she inquired from her son about the purport of the conversation, Yudhi\'f1\'f6
hira replied that from the talks of Vidura it was understood that there was a hint of fire in the house where they were proceeding. Later on, Vidura came in disguise to the P\'e4\'eb\'f2
avas and informed them that the housekeeper was going to set fire to the house on the fourteenth night of the waning moon. It was an intrigue of Dh\'e5tar\'e4\'f1\'f6ra that the P\'e4\'eb\'f2avas might die all togethe
r with their mother. And by his warning the P\'e4\'eb\'f2avas escaped through a tunnel underneath the earth so that their escape was also unknown to Dh\'e5tar\'e4\'f1\'f6
ra, so much so that after setting the fire, the Kauravas were so certain of the death of the P\'e4\'eb\'f2avas that Dh\'e5tar\'e4\'f1\'f6
ra performed the last rites of death with great cheerfulness. And during the mourning period all the members of the palace became overwhelmed with lamentation, but Vidura did not become so, because of his knowledge that the P\'e4\'eb\'f2avas were alive so
mewhere. There are many such instances of calamities, and in each of them Vidura gave protection to the P\'e4\'eb\'f2avas on one hand, and on the other he tried to restrain his brother Dh\'e5tar\'e4\'f1\'f6
ra from such intriguing policies. Therefore, he was always partial to the P\'e4\'eb\'f2avas, just as a bird protects its eggs by his wing.
