\par }\pard \qj \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid1790059 {\f35\fs28\insrsid2639816\charrsid12326592 The P\'e4\'eb\'f2avas, especially Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Yudhi\'f1\'f6hira and Arjuna, anticipated the aftereffects of the Battle of Kuruk\'f1
etra, and therefore Arjuna declined to execute the fighting. The fight was executed by the will of the Lord, but the effects of family aggrievement, as they thought of it before, had come to be true. Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Yudhi\'f1\'f6
hira was always conscious of the great plight of his uncle Dh\'e5tar\'e4\'f1\'f6ra and aunt G\'e4ndh\'e4r\'e9, and therefore he took all possible care of them in their 
old age and aggrieved conditions. When, therefore, he could not find his uncle and aunt in the palace, naturally his doubts arose, and he conjectured that they went down to the water of the Ganges. He thought himself ungrateful because when the P\'e4\'eb
\'f2avas were fatherless, Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Dh\'e5tar\'e4\'f1\'f6ra gave them all royal facilities to live, and in return he had killed all his sons in the Battle of Kuruk\'f1etra. As a pious man, Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Yudhi\'f1\'f6
hira took into account all his unavoidable misdeeds, and he never thought of the misdeeds of his uncle and company. Dh\'e5tar\'e4\'f1\'f6ra had suffered the effects of his own misdeeds by the will of the Lord, but Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Yudhi\'f1\'f6
hira was only thinking of his own unavoidable misdeeds. That is the nature of a good man and devotee of the Lord. A devotee never finds fault with others, but tries to find out his own, and thus rectify them as far as possible.}{\f35\fs28\insrsid2639816 

